00:24:13,329 --> 00:24:18,180
"Most of the solids we think of as
hard – metals, salt, ice – are crystalline.

377
00:24:18,181 --> 00:24:20,815
Their atoms are lined
up in a neat, repeating

378
00:24:20,839 --> 00:24:23,192
lattice, rather like
soldiers on parade.

379
00:24:23,193 --> 00:24:25,180
Glass isn't like that.

380
00:24:25,181 --> 00:24:29,180
When you examine its structure, the
arrangement of the atoms is random.

381
00:24:29,181 --> 00:24:31,668
It's essentially the structure
of a liquid that's been

382
00:24:31,692 --> 00:24:34,192
frozen in place before it
had time to organize itself.

383
00:24:34,193 --> 00:24:39,180
This has given rise to a famous myth,
which I'm sure some of you have heard:

384
00:24:39,479 --> 00:24:42,184
that glass is really a very
slow-moving liquid, and

385
00:24:42,208 --> 00:24:45,180
that's why old church windows
are thicker at the bottom.

386
00:24:45,181 --> 00:24:46,180
It isn't true.

387
00:24:46,181 --> 00:24:49,820
Calculations suggest that glass
at room temperature wouldn't

388
00:24:49,844 --> 00:24:53,192
flow noticeably in the
entire lifetime of the universe.

389
00:24:53,579 --> 00:24:56,419
The thick bottoms are simply
because glaziers tended to

390
00:24:56,443 --> 00:24:59,192
put the heavier edge of an
uneven pane at the bottom.

391
00:24:59,554 --> 00:25:04,180
The main ingredient of most glass
is silica, which in practice means sand.

392
00:25:04,479 --> 00:25:09,180
The difficulty is that pure silica only
melts at around 1700 degrees Celsius,

393
00:25:09,181 --> 00:25:12,180
far hotter than any
ancient furnace could reach.

394
00:25:12,181 --> 00:25:15,286
So glassmakers added soda,
that is, sodium carbonate,

395
00:25:15,310 --> 00:25:18,192
which brings the melting
point down dramatically.

396
00:25:19,030 --> 00:25:22,180
An ingredient that does
this job is known as a flux.

397
00:25:22,429 --> 00:25:26,180
And do note that word, because you'll
meet it again and again in the literature.

398
00:25:26,181 --> 00:25:29,180
But soda on its own creates a problem.

399
00:25:29,429 --> 00:25:31,180
Soda glass is chemically unstable.

400
00:25:31,181 --> 00:25:35,180
Strange as it sounds, it
will slowly dissolve in water.

401
00:25:35,181 --> 00:25:38,254
People often guess that
it's acid that attacks it, but

402
00:25:38,278 --> 00:25:41,192
in fact, ordinary rain is
quite enough, given time.

403
00:25:41,193 --> 00:25:45,180
The solution was to add
lime, which acts as a stabilizer.

404
00:25:45,181 --> 00:25:48,458
And that combination
of sand, soda, and lime is

405
00:25:48,482 --> 00:25:52,192
still the basis of about 90%
of the glass made today.

406
00:25:52,379 --> 00:25:53,180
Now to the history.

407
00:25:53,181 --> 00:25:56,514
The oldest glass objects we
know of come from Mesopotamia,

408
00:25:56,538 --> 00:25:59,192
from about four and a
half thousand years ago.

409
00:25:59,193 --> 00:26:02,069
You might imagine
cups or bottles, but those

410
00:26:02,093 --> 00:26:05,192
didn't appear for another
thousand years or so.

411
00:26:05,379 --> 00:26:08,238
The earliest objects
were beads, small, brightly

412
00:26:08,262 --> 00:26:11,192
colored, and treated
almost like precious stones.

413
00:26:12,180 --> 00:26:16,026
The real revolution came in the
first century BC, probably somewhere

414
00:26:16,050 --> 00:26:20,192
along the coast of what's now Syria,
with the invention of glass blowing.

415
00:26:21,180 --> 00:26:25,038
Instead of slowly shaping glass around
a core, a worker could gather molten

416
00:26:25,062 --> 00:26:29,192
glass on the end of a hollow tube and
blow it into shape in a matter of seconds.

417
00:26:29,193 --> 00:26:32,180
Suddenly, glass was cheap.

418
00:26:32,429 --> 00:26:35,180
The Romans adopted the
technique with great enthusiasm.

419
00:26:35,181 --> 00:26:40,180
They did make some window glass, though
it was thick and not very transparent.

420
00:26:40,429 --> 00:26:43,180
But the commonest use for
blown glass was containers.

421
00:26:43,181 --> 00:26:45,180
Now, you might expect these held wine.

422
00:26:45,181 --> 00:26:48,633
In fact, wine was mostly
kept in clay jars called

423
00:26:48,657 --> 00:26:52,192
amphorae, which were
cheaper and far less fragile.

424
00:26:52,429 --> 00:26:55,694
Glass was particularly
valued for perfume, because

425
00:26:55,718 --> 00:26:59,192
unlike clay, it doesn't
absorb the scent or alter it.

426
00:26:59,429 --> 00:27:01,848
Let's jump forward to
medieval Venice, which

427
00:27:01,872 --> 00:27:04,192
became the center of
European glassmaking.

428
00:27:04,379 --> 00:27:07,475
In 1291, the city
ordered all its glassmakers

429
00:27:07,499 --> 00:27:10,192
to move to the nearby
island of Murano.

430
00:27:10,479 --> 00:27:12,769
You'll often read that
this was done to protect

431
00:27:12,793 --> 00:27:15,192
trade secrets, and it
certainly helped with that.

432
00:27:15,229 --> 00:27:19,180
But the official reason given in
the decree was the danger of fire.

433
00:27:19,379 --> 00:27:21,974
Venice was a crowded
city full of wooden

434
00:27:21,998 --> 00:27:25,192
buildings, and the furnaces
burned day and night.

435
00:27:25,229 --> 00:27:30,192
Murano's great achievement was a glass
called cristallo, which was almost colorless.

436
00:27:30,529 --> 00:27:34,180
Ordinary glass had a greenish tint,
which comes from iron in the sand.

437
00:27:34,629 --> 00:27:37,180
It isn't caused by copper,
as is sometimes claimed.

438
00:27:37,181 --> 00:27:39,180
Copper tends to give a blue color.

439
00:27:39,729 --> 00:27:44,180
The Venetians found that adding a small
amount of manganese canceled out the green,

440
00:27:44,229 --> 00:27:48,180
which is why manganese is
sometimes nicknamed glassmaker's soap.

441
00:27:48,479 --> 00:27:54,192
In England, in the 1670s, George Ravenscroft
began adding lead oxide to his glass.

442
00:27:54,579 --> 00:27:57,314
People assume lead glass
must be stronger, but it's

443
00:27:57,338 --> 00:28:00,192
actually rather softer,
which makes it easier to cut.

444
00:28:00,779 --> 00:28:02,180
What it gained was brilliance.

445
00:28:02,181 --> 00:28:08,180
Lead makes glass bend light much more,
so when it's cut into facets, it sparkles.

446
00:28:08,181 --> 00:28:11,427
That's the origin of what
we call lead crystal, which,

447
00:28:11,451 --> 00:28:15,192
ironically, given what I said
earlier, isn't a crystal at all.

448
00:28:15,829 --> 00:28:19,180
For centuries, making flat glass
for windows remained difficult.

449
00:28:19,779 --> 00:28:23,180
You either blew a large cylinder
and cut it open, or spun a disc.

450
00:28:23,181 --> 00:28:27,180
Both methods left the glass
slightly wavy and uneven.

451
00:28:27,529 --> 00:28:30,232
The breakthrough came
in the 1950s with the float

452
00:28:30,256 --> 00:28:33,192
process, developed by
Alistair Pilkington in Britain.

453
00:28:33,193 --> 00:28:39,192
In the float process, molten glass is poured
continuously onto a bath of molten tin.

454
00:28:39,579 --> 00:28:42,290
People sometimes assume
mercury was used because it's

455
00:28:42,314 --> 00:28:45,192
liquid at room temperature,
but it boils far too easily.

456
00:28:46,180 --> 00:28:50,168
Tin stays liquid over the whole
working range, and because its surface is

457
00:28:50,192 --> 00:28:54,192
perfectly flat, the glass floating
on top ends up perfectly flat as well.

458
00:28:54,193 --> 00:28:57,180
Almost all window glass
is made this way today.

459
00:28:57,181 --> 00:29:00,180
Finally, a word about safety glass.

460
00:29:00,181 --> 00:29:03,572
Toughened glass is made
by heating ordinary glass and

461
00:29:03,596 --> 00:29:07,192
then cooling the outside
very quickly using jets of air.

462
00:29:07,529 --> 00:29:12,180
Water was tried, but it cools the surface
so violently that the glass simply cracks.

463
00:29:12,254 --> 00:29:15,474
This rapid cooling puts the
surface under compression,

464
00:29:15,498 --> 00:29:18,192
which makes the glass
several times stronger.

465
00:29:18,193 --> 00:29:21,271
And if it does break,
it shatters into small,

466
00:29:21,295 --> 00:29:24,192
blunt pieces rather
than dangerous shards.

467
00:29:24,479 --> 00:29:29,180
Next week, we'll look at more modern uses,
glass and fiber optic cables, and so on.

468
00:29:29,579 --> 00:29:31,180
So please read Chapter 6 before then.

469
00:29:32,379 --> 00:29:34,180
That is the end of the listening test.

